  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nA 1025 hPa sfc anticyclone continues to build in over NY and \nNew England this morning with clear skies and calm winds. Some \npatchy radiative mist/fog in the major and sheltered valleys \nwill burn off quickly after sunrise. After a cool start in the \n40s and 50s (it fell to 38F at Old Forge!), expect max temps to\nbe slightly warmer than yesterday with shallow mixing depths. \nExpect highs to be near or slightly below normal in the 70s to \nlower 80s with mostly sunny conditions due to the subsidence \nfrom the sfc high. Humidity levels remain comfortable. Tonight, \nmostly clear skies and light to calm winds will promote ideal \nradiative cooling conditions with fog in the sheltered valleys \nonce again. We went with a MAVMOS/NBM blend and trended cooler \nwith min temps over the northern zones with 40s to lower 50s \nnorth of the Capital Region...and mainly lower to mid 50s to the\nsouth. The upcoming weekend will feature a slight moderation \nand warming trend in temps, as the sfc anticylone slowly moves \ndownstream near the Gulf of Maine by Sunday. An isolated pop-up \nshower or t-storm is possible near the western Dacks. Otherwise,\ndewpoints in the 40s to lower 50s over the mtns, and mainly the\n50s in the valleys will be comfortable with highs near normal \nin the lower to mid 80s in the valleys/hills and 70s to around \n80F over the higher terrain which is climatologically normal for\nlate July (i.e. normal high for Albany is 84F currently). The \nfair and dry weather looks to end by Mon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA more active weather pattern sets-up Mon through the mid- \nweek. Initially, northwest flow sets-up aloft with a warm \nfront/dewpoints boundary ahead of short-wave approaching from \nfrom the Upper Midwest/western Great Lakes Region. Dewpoints \nrise into the 60s with PWATs rising to about 1-1.50\". MUCAPE\nvalue do not look that impressive on some of the medium range \nguidance and ensembles, and the shear uncertain at this time on\nMon for potential severe t-storms. A chance of showers and \nthunderstorms was kept in the forecast on Mon. The \nshower/t-storm coverage and probabilities of pcpn look even \ngreater for Tue-Tue night, as a potent short- wave trough moves \nover upstate NY and western New England. Latest NAEFS guidance \nindicates low to mid level heights fall 2-3 STDEVs below normal.\nThe cyclonic vorticity advection with the mid-level trough taps\ninto more pronounced low-level moisture. Likely to categorical \nPoPs continue in the 55-85% range on Tue. Some periods of rain \nwith showers/t-storms may be locally heavy with PWATs around 1 \nSTDEV or so above normal. 01Z/24 NBM 24-hr probs for >1\" of \nrainfall for the period ending 12Z/Wed are generally 25-45% \nacross eastern NY and western New England at this time. The \nscattered showers and isolated t-storms linger through Wed with \nthe mid and upper trough remaining near or over NY and New \nEngland. Temps begin near to slightly above normal Mon, and the \nfall slightly below normal Tue through Wed in the rain-cooled \nair/cloudier air mass. A moderation to seasonal temps to close \nJuly occurs late in the week.",
